example.paragraph:
Let $P = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      .5 & {\text{.}2} & {\text{.}3} \\
      .3 & {\text{.}8} & {\text{.}3} \\
      .2 & 0 & {\text{.}4}
      \end{bmatrix}$ and $\textbf{x}_{0} = \,\,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 \\
      0 \\
      0
      \end{bmatrix}$. Consider a system whose state is described by the Markov chain $\textbf{x}_{k + 1} = P\textbf{x}_{k}$, for $k = 0,1,\ldots$ What happens to the system as time passes? Compute the state vectors $\textbf{x}_{1},\ldots,\textbf{x}_{15}$ to find out.

statement.paragraph:
Determine if $P = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      1 & {\text{.}2} \\
      0 & {\text{.}8}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ is a regular stochastic matrix.

Is $\textbf{q} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {\text{.}6} \\
      {\text{.}8}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ a steady state vector for $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {\text{.}2} & {\text{.}6} \\
      {\text{.}8} & {\text{.}4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$? Justify your answer.

Is $\textbf{q} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      .4 \\
      .4
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ a steady state vector for $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {\text{.}2} & {\text{.}8} \\
      {\text{.}8} & {\text{.}2}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$? Justify your answer.

Is $\textbf{q} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {\text{.}6} \\
      {\text{.}4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ a steady state vector for $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {\text{.}4} & {\text{.}6} \\
      {\text{.}6} & {\text{.}4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$? Justify your answer.

Is $\textbf{q} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{l}
      {3/7} \\
      {4/7}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ a steady state vector for $A = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{rr}
      {\text{.}2} & {\text{.}6} \\
      {\text{.}8} & {\text{.}4}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$? Justify your answer.

[T] Suppose the following matrix describes the likelihood that an individual will switch between an iOS and an Android smartphone:$$\begin{array}{l}
      \begin{array}{ll}
      \begin{array}{l}
      \text{~~~~~~From} \\
      {\text{iOS}\mspace{9mu}\text{~~}\text{Android}\mspace{31mu}\text{To}}
      \end{array} & 
      \end{array} \\
      {~{\left\lbrack \begin{array}{ll}
      \text{.70} & {\mspace{27mu}\text{.15}} \\
      \text{.30} & {\mspace{27mu}\text{.85}}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack~~~~~~}\begin{array}{l}
      \text{iOS} \\
      \text{Android}
      \end{array}}
      \end{array}$$

[T] In Detroit, Hertz Rent A Car has a fleet of about $2000$ cars. The pattern of rental and return locations is given by the fractions in the table below. On a typical day, about how many cars will be rented or ready to rent from the downtown location?$$\begin{matrix}
      {\begin{matrix}
      \text{Cars~Rented~from} \\
      \begin{matrix}
      \text{City} & \text{Down-} & \text{Metro} \\
      \text{Airport} & \text{town} & \text{Airport}
      \end{matrix}
      \end{matrix}~\begin{matrix}
      \text{Returned~to}
      \end{matrix}} \\
      {\begin{bmatrix}
      {~~\text{.90}~~} & {~~\text{.01}~~} & {~~\text{.09}~~} \\
      \text{.01} & \text{.90} & \text{.01} \\
      \text{.09} & \text{.09} & \text{.90}
      \end{bmatrix}~~~\begin{array}{l}
      \text{    City~Airport} \\
      \text{    \,Downtown} \\
      \text{    Metro~Airport}
      \end{array}}
      \end{matrix}$$

Show that every $2 \times 2$ stochastic matrix has at least one steady-state vector. Any such matrix can be written in the form $P = \begin{bmatrix}
      {1 - \alpha} & \beta \\
      \alpha & {1 - \beta}
      \end{bmatrix}$, where $\alpha$ and $\beta$ are constants between $0$ and $1$. (There are two linearly independent steady-state vectors if $\alpha = \beta = 0$. Otherwise, there is only one.)

Let $S$ be the $1 \times n$ row matrix with a $1$ in each column, $S = \,\begin{bmatrix}
      1 & 1 & \cdots & 1
      \end{bmatrix}$
